void swap_value(int a,int b)
{
    int t=a; a=b; b=t;
    (void)a; (void)b;
}
void swap_address(int *a,int *b)
{
    int t=*a; *a=*b; *b=t;
}

int main(void)
{
    int a=2,b=7;
    swap_value(a,b);
    printf("After value: %d %d\n",a,b);
    swap_address(&a,&b);
    printf("After address: %d %d\n",a,b);
    return 0;
}

swap.text:
Value and address passing
Explanation in simple steps
C passes all arguments by value.
Passing ordinary values copies them, so local changes do not modify caller variables.
Passing copied addresses lets a function modify the pointed-to objects; textbooks often call this call by reference.
It supports swaps and multiple outputs, but needs valid pointers.


Algorithm
Initialize a=2,b=7.
Call swap_value: only local copies change.
Call swap_address(&a,&b): exchange caller objects.
Print after each call.


C program
#include <stdio.h>
void swap_value(int a,int b)
{
    int t=a; a=b; b=t;
    (void)a; (void)b;
}
void swap_address(int *a,int *b)
{
    int t=*a; *a=*b; *b=t;
}

int main(void)
{
    int a=2,b=7;
    swap_value(a,b);
    printf("After value: %d %d\n",a,b);
    swap_address(&a,&b);
    printf("After address: %d %d\n",a,b);
    return 0;
}

Output / trace
